75 #edb/ref 17592186411603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411612 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411612 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411612 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411612 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0e744f0f-1893-571c-a245-886e0be7796d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411614 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411614 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411614 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411614 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411614 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411614 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411614 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411614 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411614 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f22ac3a1-3b1b-5c02-9aac-e44227bf9c37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411615 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411615 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411615 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411615 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186411615 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15361c81-aace-5a26-b7c5-2e82d38f451f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411615 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.9 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & -0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.4 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.9 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 19 & -19 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186411616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473739 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186411616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473740 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186411616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473741 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186411616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411616 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186411616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62944003-1237-5928-b405-2159dbe448f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411616 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.03+0.72 \\\\ 0.07+0.18\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186411618 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186411619 #edb/ref 175921